<div class="wrap shell">
<aside class="toc" id="toc"><h4>On this page</h4><div class="grp"></div></aside>
<main>
<section id="overview">
      <h2>What Qu is</h2>
      <p>Qu (pronounced “queue”) is a specification-first language for people whose work is
        <em>numbers over time</em>: acquiring a signal, cleaning it into a table, fitting a model,
        and turning the result into a figure — often an animated one. It blends the readability of
        BASIC and MATLAB, the array discipline of FORTRAN, the ecosystem thinking of Python and R,
        the declarative scene-graph idea from POV-Ray, and the query syntax of LINQ. Above all it aims
        to read like <em>mathematics and science</em>, not like a program (§10).</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div class="tile"><strong>For scientists</strong><span>Code that looks like the math. Small scripts run with no declarations, and errors explain shape, unit, and backend problems in plain language.</span></div>
        <div class="tile"><strong>For data &amp; ML</strong><span>Frames, statistics, a uniform fit/predict/score model protocol, tensors, and reverse-mode autodiff — all sharing the array core.</span></div>
        <div class="tile"><strong>For backend engineers</strong><span>Backend-neutral source lowers to CPU, MKL, CUDA, OpenCL, or SYCL with visible fusion, memory movement, and precision policy.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note"><b>Specification first, engine later.</b> Qu 0.2 is a written language definition
        with a reference example set. This page is the human-readable companion to
        <code>docs/qu-language-spec.md</code>; the four new chapters (data, statistics, ML, animation)
        widen the DSP core of 0.1 without changing it.</p>
    </section>
<section id="tour">
      <h2>A two-minute tour</h2>
      <p>Statements are newline-oriented. Comments start with <code>#</code>. Strings interpolate with
        <code>{expr}</code>. <code>=</code> assigns; <code>:=</code> is a deferred dataflow binding the
        planner may fuse and schedule; <code>as</code> adds a type or shape contract.</p>
      <pre><code class="qu" data-src="tour"></code></pre>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div class="tile"><strong><code>=</code> vs <code>:=</code></strong><span><code>x = expr</code> evaluates now. <code>M := expr</code> records a fusible expression that becomes concrete when plotted, printed, or exported.</span></div>
        <div class="tile"><strong>Shape contracts</strong><span><code>f as vector(K, 1)</code> asserts orientation. Broadcasting is NumPy-style, but ambiguous orientation is a compile error, not a guess.</span></div>
        <div class="tile"><strong>Command syntax</strong><span>Plot verbs read like BASIC: <code>plot(t, x)</code>, <code>grid on</code>, <code>show plot</code>. Friendly to type, precise to lower.</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>
<section id="arrays">
      <h2>Arrays, ranges, broadcasting</h2>
      <p>Every numeric value is a scalar or an N-D homogeneous array; scalars are rank-0 arrays.
        Matrices are column-major by default (FORTRAN/BLAS/LAPACK friendly). Indexing is zero-based
        and slices are half-open, with a MATLAB one-based compatibility mode available per module.</p>
      <pre><code class="qu" data-src="arrays"></code></pre>
      <p>The comma-ellipsis form <code>1, 2, ..., 50</code> and the VB-style <code>1 to 20 step pi/4</code>
        both build ranges; a <code>skip eps</code> guard drops values near a singularity.</p>
    </section>
<footer>Qu draft 0.9 — specification first, engine later. Full text: <code>docs/qu-language-spec.md</code>. &nbsp;<a href="index.html">Home</a></footer>
